// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row listcomp_value_elem_type_refused (tier 3, `refuses`).
// A LEGAL list comprehension whose element VALUE has a type other than the iterator's
// element type is refused. Owner ruling 2026-09-14 on blessed divergence A6: the
// collection holds the `value` results (`expr.comprehension.list-and-map`, and
// `expr.list-comp.desugar-vec` now says "T is the type of `value`", as the map
// comprehension's K/V clause always did). Fix = this program compiles and runs, exit 3.
//
// Measured 2026-09-14 on build 0230e503bd682184 43, three spellings of the one defect
// (the element type is taken from the iterator, not from the value):
//   PART 1 (this program) — a struct value `[P { k: x } for x in src]`: rc 1 inside
//     mlir_gen, "'func.call' op operand type mismatch: expected operand type 'i64', but
//     provided '!llvm.ptr' for operand number 1". A tuple `[(x, x) for x in src]` prints
//     the same line.
//   PART 2 — a scalar value of another type, `let v: Vec<bool> = [x > 1i64 for x in src];`:
//     rc 1, "let 'v': type mismatch — expected Vec<bool>, got Vec<i64>". Legal; must
//     compile and yield a Vec<bool> of length 3.
// Formerly listcomp_nonscalar_elem_internal_verifier_diag (tier 4, `diag`, filed under the
// iterator-type reading while the owner question was open). Found 2026-09-14h while
// re-measuring hand program p2 (2026-09-10d-vecbare). Not Rust (comprehensions are A6).
package listcomp_value_elem_type_refused;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct P {
    k: i64
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let src: [i64; 3] = [1i64, 2i64, 3i64];
    let v = [P { k: x } for x in src];
    return v.len() as i32;
}
